window.__labLoaded({"id":"8.4","title":"Multiple Regression: Many Predictors at Once","goal":"Predict house price from square feet and bedrooms together, and see why the bedrooms effect disappears once size is held fixed, using the lesson's ten houses.","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Enter the ten houses","text":"Square feet, bedrooms and price (in thousands of dollars). Bigger houses tend to have more bedrooms, so the two predictors are tangled together. That is the whole point of this lab.","code":"import pandas as pd\nimport statsmodels.formula.api as smf\n\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\n    'sqft': [1000, 1200, 1400, 1600, 1800, 2000, 2200, 2400, 2600, 2800],\n    'bedrooms': [2, 2, 3, 3, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5, 6],\n    'price': [250, 280, 310, 350, 390, 420, 450, 480, 520, 550]})\n\nprint(df.corr().round(3))","out":"","figs":[]},{"title":"Bedrooms alone","text":"On its own, bedrooms seems to matter a lot: each extra bedroom goes with a large jump in price. But bedrooms may just be standing in for size.","code":"alone = smf.ols('price ~ bedrooms', data=df).fit()\n\nprint(f'bedrooms alone: {alone.params[\"bedrooms\"]:.1f} thousand per bedroom')\nprint(f'R squared = {alone.rsquared:.3f}')","out":"","figs":[]},{"title":"Both predictors together","text":"Adding sqft to the formula with + fits one model with two predictors. Each coefficient is the change in price for one more unit of that predictor while the other is held fixed.","code":"both = smf.ols('price ~ sqft + bedrooms', data=df).fit()\n\nprint(both.params.round(4))\nprint()\nprint(both.pvalues.round(4))","out":"","figs":[]},{"title":"Read the fit and what 'held fixed' means","text":"The sqft coefficient compares two houses with the same number of bedrooms and different sizes. The bedrooms coefficient compares houses of the same size with different bedrooms. R squared says how much of the price variation the pair explains.","code":"print(f'R squared          = {both.rsquared:.4f}')\nprint(f'adjusted R squared = {both.rsquared_adj:.4f}')\nprint(f'overall F = {both.fvalue:.1f}, p = {both.f_pvalue:.2e}')\n\nnew = pd.DataFrame({'sqft': [1500], 'bedrooms': [3]})\nprint(f'predicted price, 1500 sqft, 3 bed = {both.predict(new)[0]:.1f}')","out":"","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"sqft <- c(1000, 1200, 1400, 1600, 1800, 2000, 2200, 2400, 2600, 2800)\nbedrooms <- c(2, 2, 3, 3, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5, 6)\nprice <- c(250, 280, 310, 350, 390, 420, 450, 480, 520, 550)\nsummary(lm(price ~ bedrooms))\nfit <- lm(price ~ sqft + bedrooms)\nsummary(fit)\npredict(fit, data.frame(sqft = 1500, bedrooms = 3))","expect":"bedrooms alone: slope 75.6; both: Intercept 80.06, sqft 0.1668 (p < 0.0001), bedrooms 0.806 (p-value 0.872); Multiple R-squared 0.9987, Adjusted 0.9983; F-statistic 2689 on 2 and 7 DF; prediction 332.6","note":"Values are printed with more digits than this, and R shows very small p-values as '<2e-16' style numbers; the signs and sizes match Python."},"spss":{"menu":"Analyze > Regression > Linear (Dependent: price, Independents: sqft and bedrooms)","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / sqft bedrooms price.\nBEGIN DATA\n1000 2 250  1200 2 280  1400 3 310  1600 3 350  1800 4 390\n2000 4 420  2200 4 450  2400 5 480  2600 5 520  2800 6 550\nEND DATA.\nREGRESSION\n  /STATISTICS COEFF R ANOVA\n  /DEPENDENT price\n  /METHOD=ENTER sqft bedrooms.","note":"Read B and Sig. in the Coefficients table, R Square and Adjusted R Square in the Model Summary, and F in the ANOVA table."},"stata":{"code":"clear\nset obs 10\ngenerate sqft = 800 + 200 * _n\ngenerate bedrooms = real(word(\"2 2 3 3 4 4 4 5 5 6\", _n))\ngenerate price = real(word(\"250 280 310 350 390 420 450 480 520 550\", _n))\nregress price bedrooms\nregress price sqft bedrooms\ndisplay _b[_cons] + _b[sqft] * 1500 + _b[bedrooms] * 3","note":"The second regress prints the coefficients, p-values, R-squared, Adj R-squared and the overall F. sqft is built as 800 + 200 x row number, which gives 1000 to 2800 as in the lesson."},"try":["Predict the price of a 1,500 sq ft house with 5 bedrooms, then compare with 3 bedrooms. How different are they, and why so small?","Drop sqft from the formula and refit with only bedrooms. How does the bedrooms coefficient change?"]});
